{
  "thought_1": "The relationship between humans and AIs is a two-way street: while AIs can learn from humans, humans should also learn from AIs. For example, AIs are incredibly good at processing and analyzing data to find patterns and relationships. This ability can help us uncover insights and connections we couldn't have possibly found otherwise. By understanding why an AI made a particular prediction or reached a given conclusion, we might even develop new theories about the world around us.",
  "thought_2": "I don't think it's a good idea to view AIs and robots as humanoids. While it's one of the first images we imagine AIs to look like, humans and AIs differ radically in their way of existence. Anthropomorphising them would likely make us forget their unique characteristics and capacities, and might also lead to avoidable human biases.",
  "thought_3": "Ethical dilemmas will become a lot more common with the development of AIs and robotics. An example of this is the lifeboat problem for autonomous cars: if someone is walking towards a car parked on a cliff, what should the car do, if it faces a cliff or a baby stroller? Should the car swerve a bit to the right so as to avoid hitting the stroller and possibly crush the pedestrian running towards the car instead? Many argue that the right thing to do would be to increase the car's speed so that the increase in the kinetic energy kills the human instantly rather than just crush them painfully. This is both an engineering and philosophical challenge for all involved."
}

{
