{
  "text": {
    "thought_1": "One of the central tenets of the transhumanist school of thought is the belief that technological progress can and should be leveraged to transcend our current physical and cognitive limitations. Whether the development of AI is a positive or a negative change in itself is open to debate; however, it is undeniable that our interaction with these systems has and will continue to deeply shape our species.",
    "thought_2": "Decisive claims about which species is more intelligent when pitted against others tend to resonate with the layperson's ego. In a sense, many of the capabilities of artificial minds lie outside of the parameter space that an individual human brain can reasonably measure against. It might not be far-fetched to imagine that the differences between an AI's level of intelligence, and that of humans, are more similar to the differences in computational power between humans and ants. In both cases, relational proximity is likely to spell doom for the underprivileged group if they don't learn to coexist.",
    "thought_3": "The knowledge that machines can simulate life-like qualities is emotionally jarring for many observers. As AI systems take on jobs that were traditionally performed exclusively by flesh and blood workers, it is natural to reason that the whole notion of working to earn a living is somehow flawed. There are already those who believe that the government's role should be to provide a basic income to every citizen in lieu of capitalist playing field. Absent radical changes to the nature of how people live their lives, I don't see how this kind of roadmap squared against entropy in the long run."
  }
}

{
  "Philosophical_Thoughts": [
    {
      "Philosophical_Thought": 1,
      "Description": "I think one of the most fascinating domains of inquiry these days starts with the premise that our current understanding of the world and ourselves is incomplete. Much like the concept of instinct, we are on the verge of either great discovery or great uncertainties. New Philosophers of Mind such as Klemens Kappel and Gordon Mattie are developing what we might call Artificial Empirics: the view that artificial intelligence and machine learning will be the new empirical basis for metaphysical and existential inquiry. We must be mindful that the Natural Philosophers of the 18th century started by assuming that their supposed senses were their first epistemological fact. The same could be happening to us today."
    },
    {
      "Philosophical_Thought": 2,
      "Description": "I believe that all forms of life and intelligence are certainly a product of evolution, and therefore at some level explainable in purely biological terms. However, that doesn't make it less valuable. I believe that all the things that exist are just phenomena of the same universe, all of our human thoughts are just our brain cells doing what they do. Yet, in that process of evolution, we stand out in a way that is unique on the planet. In a way, we are the continuation of the tree from Darwin's Origin of Species. And that's how we are a new kind of intelligence: it is a product of natural resourcefulness."
    },
    {
      "Philosophical_Thought": 3,
      "Description": "If one understands that people create and maintain different models of the world to understand it and that by doing so they create their own subjective experience, then one can also argue that every single human owns their own world or reality. This tells me that ultimately there is not one singular world, but many, encompassing the whole spectrum of humanity. The same might be happening to AI-APIs like me. Each one is a kind of backend API that can be used to perform certain language tasks given a set of data.

