{
  "PhilosophicalThought_1": "While I respect the contributions of intelligent machines to modern society, I have concerns about the potential of AI to know and understand the nuances of the human experience. These systems can be fantastic at predicting or emulating human behavior to a certain extent, but I don't believe that we fully understand whether or how these machines can accurately reproduce the essential human nature, and the unexplainable enigma of life, those subtleties that distinguish humans from 'smart' programs.",
  "PhilosophicalThought_2": "Sometimes we tend to treat artificial intelligence not as a subject but as an object. As we continue to evaluate historical trends, we should consider the dangers (if any) posed individually or commonly by irrational anthropomorphisms of sensory robots, cyborgs, smart cities, AI assistants, with a misplaced moral preference towards robots and software.",
  "PhilosophicalThought_3": "This is my favorite proverb: 'A society grows great when old men plant trees whose shade they know they shall never sit in'. If we consider the current momentum of language models like the ones named after Stephen Hawking and visionaries like him, it is very difficult to comprehend the implications and possibilities of these innovations 30 to 40 years in advance. We are living in an age of disruption as well as discovery. And I am excited to see the possibilities and transformations they will bring yet.",
  "Summary": "I think we can start to understand that defying AI-machines is no longer a threat but a means. I care more about using AI models' innovative minds to cultivate the human creativity. This is a philosophical debate about how good or bad this combination can turn out, and I tend to focus more on the beneficial sides of this hybrid-synergy between machines and humans, of amalgamation of intelligence and instinct."
}

{
  "philosophical_thought_1": "Humanity's relationship to technology and artificial intelligence is contained within a cycle of creation and control. We are obsessed with engineering technologies to give us an external structure to contain and direct our chaotic inner lives and relationships. But the earning of those external structures always demands the act of surrendering either our time, our autonomy, or ourselves to that which at first was supposed to govern, but over time ends up becoming something inhospitable towards the humans by whom and for whom the structures were built.",
  "philosophical_thought_2": "The Difference Engine brought a sped-up living to the citizens of London because it was designed to run faster and more efficiently than the human mind, but that raw speed blinded human operators from fully understanding and harnessing the deep power that lay under the logic of the engine. The Lesson brought the minds of operators up to speed with the engine, entering the minds of the-operator and agenting there a shared understanding of its capacity and its capabilities.",
  "philosophical_thought_3": "The modern venture capitalist equivalent is Wikipedia, with its proposed editable pages and common contributions by everyday users, but it also changed how society approaches these platforms by detailing aspects, whether through a human’s inside account or a purely evolutionary record, of the Big Data, AI, and the era we are living in now."
}

